00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.510 align:start position:0%
 
in this video i'm going to be going  through five tech side project ideas you  can start right now without writing any  lines of code if you're new to this  channel i'm a solutions marketer  graduate based in australia working at  aws  and in this video i'm going to be going  through these five side projects  i'll be talking about how to get started  how hard it is to get started  and also give you a personal rating out  of five because these were side projects  that i've done to varying degrees during  my time in university so these side  projects will also come in handy if  you're a student applying to tech  companies for non-technical roles  because employers look for people who  have a strong interest in tech  and si projects are a great way to  demonstrate that this is a video i wish  i had during my first year of university  so without further ado let's jump right  into it okay so idea number one is  starting a podcast  and this is a very very powerful one  because you might be asking me and you  might be wondering  well lucy don't i need to be an expert  in something related to tech to start a  tech podcast  but in reality most people who start a  podcast don't have much experience in  what they want to talk about  because they're initially interested in  the topic and they want to learn more  about it  but what they do is instead of talking  about the topic themselves at the start  they bring a subject matter experts onto  their show and the good thing about this  is for example if you start a podcast  related to  getting people into product management  so you interview people who  are senior product managers you bring  them on to the show become an active  listener and ask them about their  experiences  what's really helpful is that you're not  only gaining these experiences and  learning about them but you're also  sharing it to other students who are  interested in the same thing  so starting a podcast isn't as  intimidating as it might seem  so you start off by defining your niche  and then you gather equipment whether  you want to use your iphone microphone  or investing in an external microphone  and then you find guests for your  podcast so the best way to do this is to  reach out to people on linkedin by  sending a personalized message  and afterwards blocking out one or two  hours to record the podcast with the  guest either in person or  zoom editing the podcast using an audio  editor  and finally uploading it onto youtube  spotify or anchor.fm which helps you  distribute it across multiple channels  some potential features directions  include adding video to your podcast  monetizing it and seeking out brand  sponsorships  now side project idea number two is  starting a tech youtube channel which is  what you're watching right now  so i started this channel towards the  end of last year when i was doing intern  at aws  and it's been pretty fun so far it's  definitely had its fair share of  challenges  so for my first video it took me over 10  hours to make  and a lot of the time i feel like giving  up and just not making videos anymore  but it's been super worthwhile and i  feel like it's more like a long-term  investment but one thing i regret doing  is not starting this earlier so last  year i already received my grad offer  and already knew that i was going to be  at aws this year and there was just a  lot of certainty and i felt like i was  ready to start a channel but  in reality i think what you should do is  start when you're not ready when you  don't feel prepared when you don't have  the qualifications and you're not an  intern at a tech company and you want to  document your experience getting there  so document your journey share  experience with others because there's  so many other people going through the  same experience  and they will resonate a lot with their  content and it's something you can bring  up  during interviews when you talk about  your experiences creating tech videos  for students  another branch of youtube you can dive  into is making tutorials so anything  you've learned in your university  classes anything that stands out you can  teach that through short videos  and publish it onto youtube so very  similar to starting a podcast  you begin by defining your niche and  gathering equipment and then  brainstorming your video ideas  after that you record and edit your  videos and upload it onto youtube  some potential future directions include  monetizing it  in that case you need 1 000 subscribers  and 4 000 hours of watch time  you can also try to get brand  sponsorships and experiment by hosting  live streams tech side project id number  three  is forming a tech community now this is  one of my most favorite ones because  it's the one that  has really defined my university  experiences i think it's  one thing to join a community that  already exists but it's a whole new  thing  to create something from the ground up  and building that  bring together like-minded people to  share resources attend events  and having that community where you're  all working towards a similar goal  is just so rewarding during my time in  university i started a student society  with a couple of friends  it's called the unsw digital society and  during a one year period we held over 45  events  and most importantly we established  something that can sustain itself  indefinitely so even though my friends  and i so the co-founders were not part  of the society anymore we were able to  hand it over to other students who are  part of the organizing committee and so  they can continue on and keep making  impact on the student community  so studying a tech club involves a bit  of planning because you're not only  responsible for yourself and your own  content  but you're also responsible for others  and that involves having a very clear  vision of the club  after all the logistics and planning the  next step would be to gather a group of  people  and create a committee after that you'll  be setting up a community online whether  that be on facebook or meetup  or an instagram page and then launching  your first event you can tailor the rest  of your events based on the feedback you  receive  and make sure you promote your events  through word of mouth and different  channels  potential future directions include  expanding out to different audiences  creating a mentoring program and even  creating custom merchandise  so side project idea number four is case  competitions or hackathons  and i think i'm cheating a little bit  with this one because it doesn't really  count as a side project per se  it's more like you bring a group of  people together and you participate in  something that helps you create a thai  project  when you have 24 hours to come up with a  solution with a team  really flesh it out come up with  prototypes you go through this whole  design thinking process and  at times even the implementation as well  and i think that it's a really good idea  for you to participate in  a lot of different hackathons because  not only do you get to develop your  communication skills and teamwork skills  but there's also a possibility that the  idea that you come up with during the  hackathon is something that you want to  work on in the future as well so to join  hackathons it's actually very easy  because these days many of the  hackathons are online  and you don't have to go to a physical  location now to compete so step one is  to find the hackathon you want to  participate in  step two is to form the group make sure  you have different skill sets in the  team  and step three is to participate in the  competition if you feel like the  hackathon idea has really strong  potential you could turn it into a slide  project  or even one day host your own hackathon  the final side project idea is creating  a personal website using a website  builder and you can disagree with me in  the comments if you want  but my personal opinion on this is that  personal websites are pretty overrated i  think in the past it was pretty common  for a lot of people to have their own  personal website  um and they can set it up have like  these cool themes and everything like  that  um but i think nowadays there is just so  much reach  in platforms such as linkedin youtube  and instagram  i think as a student if you're  interested in ux design um anything  related to  photography or anything art related it's  definitely helpful to have a  personal website because that's where  you create your own portfolio  and you can showcase that but i think if  you apply to any other role  i don't really see the point in having a  personal website if you can just  use linkedin to display all experiences  document your journey onto youtube  share your photos onto instagram instead  of posting it onto  your personal website i see a lot more  value in that because companies and  students they can reach you in a more  effective way so  taking youtube for example youtube is  the world's second largest search engine  which means if you publish a video going  like my journey interning at  this company and you document that  experience it's much easier for someone  to find it compared to if you just  uploaded it onto your personal website  so that's just my personal opinion on  this  but i think it's still very worthwhile  to have a personal website and that's  why i decided to mention this  idea at the very end i would recommend  focusing on the other four  and if you have time you can create a  personal website and  sort of link everything together with  that  thanks so much for watching i hope you  got something out of it please give this  video a like and subscribe for more tech  career and student advice bye for now

